Step 7: Build Remaining Screens

Screen 4 (Confirmation): Duplicate Screen 2, add small banner at top with checkmark + "Task added successfully"
Screen 5 (Edit Task): Duplicate Screen 3, change title to "Edit Task", add trash icon, add "Delete" button
Screen 6 (Settings): Use Rectangle and Text tools to create list items with section headers
Step 8: Add Annotations (Optional but Helpful)

Create new layer above all frames
Use Text tool to add notes:
Label interactive elements: "Tap to add task"
Explain states: "Checkbox - empty state"
Note sizes: "48px tap target for accessibility"
Draw arrows (Line tool) connecting annotations to elements
Step 9: Organize Your Layers

In left panel, rename all layers clearly:
"Header Bar", "Task Card 1", "Add Button", etc.
Group related elements:
Select multiple items (Shift + Click)
Right-click → Group Selection (Cmd/Ctrl + G)
Name groups: "Header Group", "Form Fields Group"
Step 10: Final Polish

Align elements properly:
Select items → Right panel → use alignment tools
Check consistent spacing (use spacebar to measure)
Ensure all text is readable:
Check contrast (dark text on white, white text on dark)
Make sure tap targets are at least 44x44px (accessibility standard)
Review all frames - zoom out to see full flow
7. Design Thinking Process (Brief)
Empathize
Conduct interviews or observations to understand student behaviors around task management—watch when they write down assignments, 
note their frustrations, and listen for the language they use to describe academic workload. Simulated research (like the interviews above) 
helps you step into users' shoes and identify real pain points rather than assumed problems.

Define
Synthesize research findings into a clear problem statement that captures WHO is affected (college students),
 WHAT they struggle with (managing academic deadlines), and WHY current solutions fail (too complex or not tailored to academic context). 
Your persona (Alex) should embody these insights in a memorable, human way.

Ideate
Sketch multiple solutions quickly without judgment—consider different ways to organize tasks
  (by course, by date, by priority), various input methods (quick-add, voice, template), and notification strategies. 
Low-fidelity wireframes let you explore ideas rapidly before committing to detailed design.

Prototype
Create clickable wireframes in Figma by linking frames together (use Prototype mode: drag from button to 
next screen)—this allows you to test the core flow (adding a task) without building actual code. Keep it simple: 
just connect the "+" button to the Add Task screen, then "Save" back to Home.

Test
Share your prototype with 3-5 target users (classmates, study partners) and ask them to complete one task:
  "Add an assignment for English class due next Friday"—observe where they hesitate, what they tap that isn't interactive, 
  and what confuses them. One round of testing will reveal clarity issues in your wireframes that you can fix before final submission.
